WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:06.170
We love superhero movies, but sometimes filmmakers
seem to think that because the movies feature

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.179
superheroes, they don't have to feature logic
or reason.

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:13.839
From hiding critical clues in the stupidest
places to plain old boneheaded plot holes,

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:18.690
here are some superhero movie moments that
make absolutely no sense.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.870
Howard Stark's terrible clues

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:25.150
Iron Man 2 was bloated to begin with, but
the big twist was one of the dumbest plot

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.590
points possible.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:30.660
Tony discovers the new element he needs at
just the right time thanks to clues hidden

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.260
by his dear old dead dad, Howard.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.429
"I built this for you and some day you'll
realize it represents a whole lot more than

00:00:38.429 --> 00:00:39.429
people's inventions.

00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:41.859
It represents my life's work."

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:46.780
That's incredibly lucky timing, especially
considering Howard hid his clues in one of

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.379
the most random ways possible, embedding a
cryptic message to Tony in an old home movie

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:56.660
reel — which then unlocks an even more cryptic
clue hidden inside the old Stark EXPO design.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.960
But considering the clues were being kept
top secret by Nick Fury himself, couldn't

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.250
Howard have just, you know… written Tony
a letter with the info?

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.940
Or maybe just a note on the fridge?

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.920
That would've been way, way easier to find.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.229
Why not use Quicksilver all the time?

00:01:12.229 --> 00:01:16.170
Introducing a speedster can make for awesome
effects and cool set pieces.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:21.640
But it also creates more than a few plot problems,
as viewers of X-Men: Days of Future Past found

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.640
out.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.030
Sure, the Quicksilver super-speed scene is
easily the coolest moment in a very good movie.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:31.880
But, why on Earth would Professor X sideline
Quicksilver for the rest of the movie after

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.520
getting a peek at his abilities?

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.000
Quicksilver can take out every enemy in the
movie before they even know they're in a fight.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.920
But after getting Quicksilver to help free
Magneto, Xavier and future Wolverine just…

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.500
send him back to his basement for the rest
of the film.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.470
That seems not so smart.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.240
Why wait for the House Party?

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.659
Fans might be mixed on Iron Man 3, but there's
no denying the film did a great job of putting

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:58.869
Tony Stark outside his comfort zone and telling
a story unlike pretty much every other Iron

00:01:58.869 --> 00:01:59.869
Man movie.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:04.090
We just have one head-scratcher: Why didn't
he just pull out the "House Party" protocol

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.609
sooner and get that army of super-suits out
of their hidey holes?

00:02:07.609 --> 00:02:12.650
He spends most of the film trying to repair
and reacquire his latest suit, but he apparently

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:17.260
has an emergency protocol all this time that
can get an army of autonomous Iron Guys in

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.260
the air.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.860
Why not flip that lever a while back?

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.290
Yes, they needed to excavate the mansion to
get a path cleared, but it doesn't take that

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.599
long to just cut a man-sized hole down to
the lab, does it?

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:30.069
Logos without logic

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:34.650
It's hard to deny that Batman v Superman:
Dawn of Justice was a bloated mess, and one

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.790
of the film's biggest problems is that it
tries to shoehorn in all sorts of setup for

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.459
Justice League at the expense of the Batman
versus Superman story.

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:44.090
The most ridiculous example?

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.990
When Diana is checking out Lex Luthor's super
secret disk drive, she finds files on pretty

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:53.629
much all the future Justice League members
— complete with little logos that Lex apparently

00:02:53.629 --> 00:02:57.970
designed for them in his free time — and
then spends five minutes watching the equivalent

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:02.140
of a bunch of YouTube clips while Gotham and
Metropolis are being ravaged by super-villains.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.060
C'mon, lady, focus up!

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.970
The 'Francis' conundrum

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:11.560
One of the big jokes in Deadpool is the fact
that Wade sneakily figures out that Ajax's

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.780
real name is Francis, then proceeds to call
him that just to piss him off.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.060
"With a name like Francis…"

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.060
"Francis?"

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.360
"That's his legal name.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.940
He got Ajax from the dish soap."

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.920
When Wade gets loose later and sets off on
his revenge mission, he starts hunting down

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:32.370
all of Ajax's known associates by asking for
Francis.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:33.370
It's great!

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:34.370
And funny!

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:38.180
Except since the entire point is that Ajax
doesn't want anyone to know his real name,

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:42.390
none of the people Deadpool is torturing should
have any idea who he's talking about.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:46.709
If Deadpool had simply told any of them that
he was looking for Ajax, rather than Francis,

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:49.910
maybe the whole movie could have been wrapped
up in under 15 minutes.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:52.200
Fortunately for fans, it wasn't.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.780
Batman's healing factor

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.130
Christopher Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy is
one of the seminal film franchises in comic

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:02.660
book history, but even the mighty Nolan can
stretch things a bit too far.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:07.290
Case in point: the fact that old man Batman
in The Dark Knight Rises is somehow stronger

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:11.890
and more physically capable than any version
of the Dark Knight we've seen up until then,

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.970
despite having a busted knee and a broken
back.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.859
Not to mention he's been a retired invalid
for almost a decade, meaning he should be

00:04:18.859 --> 00:04:20.520
woefully out of shape.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:25.750
Yet, one appointment with a prison chiropractor
later, and Bruce goes from super couch potato

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.090
to unbeatable.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:29.460
One popular orb

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.520
Marvel's Guardians the Galaxy is so darn fun
that it's hard to actually nitpick much of

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:35.740
anything.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.490
But watch it a few times and the seams start
to show.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.889
One of the biggest is the point that actually
kicks off the whole movie: Star-Lord's trip

00:04:42.889 --> 00:04:47.180
to Morag to retrieve that orb, which is actually
an Infinity Stone.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:51.220
If this thing is in such high demand, why
hasn't anyone retrieved it before?

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.840
And what kind of crazy coincidence is it that
after being ignored for untold centuries,

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.900
everyone literally shows up at the exact same
moment looking for it?

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.639
"How do you know about this?"

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:04.710
I don't even know it is, I'm just a junker,
man.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.310
Just che-checking stuff out."

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:10.770
Director James Gunn tried to handwave this
in the commentary track, saying the planet

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:15.150
has tidal seasons that last hundreds of years,
so everyone is showing up because this is

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:18.530
the first time the orb hasn't been underwater
in 300 years.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:23.440
That unlikely explanation might get him a
No-Prize from Marvel, but since it's not actually

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.270
in the movie, it doesn't really count.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:27.270
"Congratulations!

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.910
You're another lucky no-prize winner.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.289
You'll find it enclosed, enclosed.

00:05:32.289 --> 00:05:33.930
They'd open it up, the envelope was empty."

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.690
Thanks for watching!

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:38.530
